Uses count only inside the declaring procedure: nCursor read in Second
does not use the nCursor declared in First (diag.unused_variable). A
:PUBLIC name is counted file-wide, so gTrayCount read in Second keeps its
declaration quiet; the :PUBLIC itself fires diag.limit_public_vars.
-- input.ssl --
:PUBLIC gTrayCount;
:PROCEDURE First;
	:DECLARE nCursor;
:ENDPROC;
:PROCEDURE Second;
	:DECLARE nCursor;
	nCursor := gTrayCount + 1;
	:RETURN nCursor;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PUBLIC gTrayCount;
:PROCEDURE First;
	:DECLARE nCursor;
:ENDPROC;

:PROCEDURE Second;
	:DECLARE nCursor;
	nCursor := gTrayCount + 1;
	:RETURN nCursor;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
1:1-1:8 info limit_public_vars: ':PUBLIC' variables persist across procedures and risk namespace pollution. Prefer ':DECLARE' with parameter passing
3:11-3:18 hint unused_variable: Variable 'nCursor' is declared but never used
